\begin_layout Chapter
Qubit Visualization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The traditional approach to teaching quantum computing typically requires the learner to first acquire a substantial body of formal mathematics,
 including linear algebra,
 complex numbers,
 and probability theory,
 before any intuition about the behavior of qubits can be developed.
 This sequencing places a considerable barrier between the learner and the subject matter,
 and often results in the physical and conceptual significance of concepts such as superposition and entanglement being obscured by the notation required to describe them.
 The pedagogical premise underlying the present work is that this barrier can be substantially lowered by inverting the customary order of exposure:
 rather than deriving intuition from formalism,
 the learner is first placed in direct,
 repeated,
 and low stakes contact with the phenomena themselves,
 with the formal description introduced only afterwards,
 as an explanation of what has already been observed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Play is intrinsically motivating,
 and therefore sustains attention over the repeated trials that are necessary for an unfamiliar and counter-intuitive concept to become familiar.
 Play is also forgiving of error:
 within the context of a game,
 an unexpected or seemingly paradoxical outcome is not experienced as a failure to be avoided,
 but as an invitation to further exploration,
 in a manner that is considerably more difficult to reproduce within a formal instructional setting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As we have seen in the past chapter to learn more about quantum entanglement I have created different circuits,
 like 
\emph on
levels in a video game
\emph default
;
 to discover better the behaviors behind different configurations of circuits.
 In the following pages I'll show you the visualizations for each experiments explaining the different properties observed in the simulation and also running the code on the real quantum computer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To visualize the data I have used different Python modules,
 explained in details in the next chapter.
 Not always will be possible to see the full complexity of all the aspects of qubits.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this experiments I'm trying to emphasize only the entanglement state between two qubits;
 but to be more exhaustive and intellectually honest I have to mention also that is possible to establish the entanglement state with more then two qubits.
 It's also necessary to specify to the reader that since the two qubits are connected some operations characteristics of the qubits will not be visible.
 To show this compromise about learning and playing,
 I have deliberately chosen to don't create any entanglement in the first experiment,
 as way to make the reader consciousness about how is appearing a qubit in a Bloch sphere.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Reading graphs:
 complex numbers,
 vectors,
 and the Bloch sphere
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To better understand the graph and chart produced during the experiments I add here a simple reminder of the concepts need to generate the charts and understand it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Complex numbers as vectors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/ch3_complex_numbers_as_vectors.png
	lyxscale 35
	scale 35

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align right
[
\family typewriter
ch3_complex_numbers_as_vectors.png
\family default
:
 graph of a complex number as vector]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace{1mm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A complex number can be described,
 for the purposes of this thesis,
 in exactly the same terms as an ordinary vector in physics:
 a magnitude,
 and a direction.
 Figure [
\family typewriter
ch3_complex_numbers_as_vectors.png
\family default
] illustrates this correspondence.
 A complex number a + bi is drawn as an arrow,
 denoted v,
 originating from the origin of a plane;
 its length,
 denoted |v| and referred to as its magnitude,
 and its direction,
 given by the angle the arrow makes with the horizontal axis,
 together fully specify it.
 This is the same descriptive language used for vectors elsewhere in physics,
 and it is the only language required to interpret the amplitudes appearing throughout this thesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Describing a vector by its numbers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alongside the picture of an arrow,
 a vector can be written just as simply as a short list of plain numbers,
 and this is the form in which vectors actually appear in the figures generated by the game.
 For a vector drawn in a plane,
 two numbers are enough:
 one telling how far along the horizontal axis the arrow reaches,
 and one telling how far along the vertical axis it reaches.
 This is exactly what is meant,
 for example,
 by a line such as |00⟩:
 −0.49+0.00i,
 printed in several of the figures:
 it states that the arrow representing that particular amplitude reaches −0.49 along the horizontal axis,
 and 0.00 along the vertical axis,
 that is,
 it points directly along the horizontal axis,
 in the negative direction,
 at a distance of 0.49 from the origin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The vector used on the Bloch sphere works in the same way,
 except that three numbers are needed instead of two,
 one for each of the three directions in which the sphere extends.
 These three numbers are what the game computes internally in order to draw the arrow shown in each Bloch sphere picture,
 and they are also what allows the length of that arrow to be measured:
 the longer the arrow,
 the larger these numbers are,
 and when the arrow shrinks to the centre of the sphere,
 all three numbers approach zero.
 The value printed as |v| in the figures is precisely this length,
 obtained from those three numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Vector length and probability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/ch3_vector_length_and_probability.png
	lyxscale 35
	scale 35

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align right
[
\family typewriter
ch3_vector_length_and_probability.png
\family default
:
 vectors and probability]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace{1mm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each of the two basic states of a qubit,
 |0⟩ and |1⟩,
 appears in a superposition together with a vector of this kind,
 referred to as an amplitude.
 The rule connecting an amplitude to anything observable concerns only its magnitude:
 the probability of obtaining a given outcome upon measurement is equal to the square of the magnitude of the corresponding vector.
 This is illustrated in the right-hand panel of figure [
\family typewriter
ch3_vector_length_and_probability.png
\family default
],
 where vectors of increasing length are shown alongside the probability obtained by squaring that length;
 a vector of magnitude 0.71,
 repeatedly encountered in the figures generated by the game,
 corresponds to a probability of approximately 0.50.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important consequence of this rule,
 illustrated in the left-hand panel of the same figure,
 is that the probability depends only on the length of the vector,
 and not on its direction.
 The three example vectors shown are of equal length but point in three different directions,
 and all three consequently correspond to the same probability of 0.50.
 The direction of the vector,
 referred to as its phase,
 is therefore not reflected in the probability value alone;
 it constitutes separate information,
 which will be shown in later sections to be responsible for distinguishing between certain quantum states that would otherwise appear identical.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Bloch sphere
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/ch3_bloch_sphere_illustration.png
	lyxscale 35
	scale 35

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align right
[
\family typewriter
ch3_bloch_sphere_illustration.png
\family default
:
 the Bloch sphere]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace{1mm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Bloch sphere,
 shown in figure [
\family typewriter
ch3_bloch_sphere_illustration.png
\family default
],
 extends this vector description to the state of a qubit considered as a whole.
 The state is represented as a single vector drawn from the centre of a sphere to a point on its surface:
 a vector pointing to the top of the sphere corresponds to the state |0⟩,
 a vector pointing to the bottom corresponds to the state |1⟩,
 and,
 as shown in the figure,
 a vector lying on the equator,
 midway between the two,
 corresponds to an equal blend of both.
 Every direction on the sphere corresponds to a possible state of the qubit,
 with the two poles representing the two definite classical outcomes and every other point representing some intermediate superposition of them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The length of this vector is equally significant,
 and constitutes the detail of greatest relevance to the figures presented in this thesis.
 A vector reaching the surface of the sphere indicates that the qubit possesses a fully defined state of its own.
 When two qubits become entangled,
 however,
 neither of them retains such a state considered individually;
 the state can only be properly described by referring to the pair as a whole.
 This circumstance is reflected,
 in the figures generated by the game,
 by a visible shortening of the corresponding vector,
 down to the exact centre of the sphere in the case of maximal entanglement,
 at which point the vector vanishes entirely.
 The presence of a full-length vector therefore indicates that a qubit is not entangled,
 while a shortened or vanished vector indicates that it is,
 with the degree of shortening reflecting the extent of the entanglement involved.
\end_layout

