\begin_layout Subsection
Experiment 1:
 two independent qubits in absence of entanglement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finding a couple of cards with the same emoji in the first experiments show us a graphical view of two qubits without entanglement as follow:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/exp1_ch3_visualization1.png
	lyxscale 30
	scale 30

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align right
[
\family typewriter
exp1_ch3_visualization1.png
\family default
:
 two independent qubits]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace{1mm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The uppermost region presents a summary of the quantum state associated with the two qubits involved.
 The individual states of qubit A and qubit B are given explicitly,
 in the form cos(θ)|0⟩ + sin(θ)|1⟩,
 with the rotation angle of qubit B differing from that of qubit A by π,
 in accordance with the antipodal relationship established between the two qubits in this experiment.
 Immediately below,
 the concurrence of the pair is reported as exactly zero,
 together with an explicit indication that the two qubits are not entangled,
 and a noise index of zero per cent,
 confirming that the state was generated by an ideal,
 noiseless simulation rather than by execution on physical hardware.
 The final line of this region reports the four complex amplitudes of the joint two-qubit state in the computational basis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The central region displays the Bloch sphere representation of each of the two qubits individually.
 Both vectors are reported with unit magnitude,
 denoted |v| = 1.00,
 indicating that each qubit occupies a pure state;
 the two vectors are observed to point in diametrically opposite directions,
 a direct visual consequence of the angular relationship imposed between them.
 This is to be contrasted with the qubit visualizations produced in the entangled experiments,
 in which the corresponding vectors are of reduced magnitude,
 or vanish entirely in the case of maximal entanglement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rightmost region presents the measurement probabilities of each qubit individually in the computational basis,
 obtained directly from the corresponding Bloch vector.
 The values displayed,
 approximately 0.38 and 0.62 for qubit 0 and their exact reverse for qubit 1,
 are found to be numerically consistent with the rotation angles reported in the upper region,
 and are symmetric between the two qubits as a further consequence of their antipodal relationship.
 The absence of any bar corresponding to a joint or correlated outcome is notable,
 and distinguishes this panel from the joint-probability representations employed in the entangled experiments,
 reflecting the fact that,
 in the absence of entanglement,
 each qubit possesses an independently meaningful probability distribution of its own.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taken together,
 the figure constitutes direct visual evidence,
 obtained from the running application rather than from a purely theoretical construction,
 of a separable two-qubit state:
 two qubits in well-defined,
 individually observable configurations,
 exhibiting no measurable correlation,
 and no entanglement as quantified by the reported concurrence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Experiment 2:
 randomly and partially entangled
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/exp2_ch3_visualization1.png
	lyxscale 30
	scale 30

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align right
[
\family typewriter
exp3_ch3_visualization1.png
\family default
:
 two partially entangled qubits]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace{1mm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The figure reproduces a screenshot of the graphical interface generated by Experiment 2 following the identification of a matching pair of cards during actual execution of the application.
 The window is titled "Entangled Pair —
 Random Partial Entanglement," and is organized into the same three regions employed throughout the figures presented in this thesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The uppermost region reports the parameters and outcome of the underlying circuit.
 The rotation angle θ,
 here equal to 0.55 radians and fixed once for the emoji associated with this pair,
 is given together with the corresponding pre-encoding state,
 cos(0.28)|00⟩ + sin(0.28)|11⟩.
 The concurrence of the resulting pair is reported as 0.53,
 explicitly labelled as this particular pair's fixed degree of entanglement,
 together with a noise index of zero per cent,
 confirming that the state was obtained from an ideal,
 noiseless simulation.
 The final line reports the four complex amplitudes of the joint two-qubit state;
 it may be observed that the amplitude is concentrated on the outcomes |01⟩ and |10⟩,
 rather than |00⟩ and |11⟩ as the pre-encoding formula would suggest,
 a consequence of the local bit-flip operation applied during the encoding of the card's sign,
 discussed in the preceding sections.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The central region displays the Bloch sphere representation of the two qubits individually.
 In contrast to the case of separable qubits,
 the two vectors are here observed to be of reduced length,
 each reported as |v| = 0.85,
 rather than reaching the surface of the sphere.
 This value is consistent with the reported concurrence,
 the two quantities being related in such a way that an increase in one corresponds to a decrease in the other;
 a partially entangled pair is thus seen to correspond to a partially shortened vector,
 of intermediate length between the fully defined vectors of a separable qubit and the vanishing vectors of a maximally entangled pair.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rightmost region presents the joint outcome probabilities,
 that is,
 the probabilities associated with the four possible results of measuring both qubits together,
 rather than the probabilities of each qubit considered individually.
 The distribution is found to be concentrated principally on the outcome |01⟩,
 with probability 0.93,
 and to a lesser extent on the outcome |10⟩,
 with probability 0.07,
 while the outcomes |00⟩ and |11⟩ are found to occur with negligible probability.
 These values are consistent with the squared magnitudes of the corresponding amplitudes reported in the upper region.
 The presence of two,
 rather than four,
 distinguishable outcomes with appreciable probability constitutes a further indication of the correlation existing between the two qubits,
 in contrast with the case of a separable pair,
 for which all four outcomes would in general occur with comparable probability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Experiment 3:
 one of the four Bell states generated
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ../img/exp3_ch3_visualization1.png
	lyxscale 30
	scale 30

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align right
[
\family typewriter
exp3_ch3_visualization1.png
\family default
:
 Bell state generator]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
hspace{1mm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The figure reproduces a screenshot of the graphical interface generated by Experiment 3 following the identification of a matching pair of cards during actual execution of the application.
 The window is titled "Bell State Explorer Φ+/Φ-/Ψ+/Ψ-," and departs from the layout employed in the preceding experiments in that the two Bloch sphere representations are here replaced by a color coded representation of the density matrix associated with the generated state,
 positioned alongside the joint outcome probability chart.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The uppermost region identifies the specific Bell state produced on this occasion as Ψ⁻ = (|01⟩−|10⟩)/√2,
 together with a concurrence of 1.00,
 explicitly labelled as corresponding to an entangled pair,
 and a noise index of zero per cent,
 confirming that the state was obtained from an ideal,
 noiseless simulation.
 The four complex amplitudes of the joint state are reported in the final line,
 and are found to be consistent with the stated formula,
 with non-zero amplitude,
 of equal magnitude and opposite sign,
 associated with the outcomes |01⟩ and |10⟩.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The density matrix representation,
 shown in the lower left,
 employs a grid in which each cell corresponds to one entry of the matrix,
 indexed by a pair of basis states,
 |00⟩,
 |01⟩,
 |10⟩,
 and |11⟩.
 The size of each colored square is proportional to the magnitude of the corresponding matrix entry,
 while its color indicates the sign of that entry,
 blue denoting a positive value and red a negative value.
 In the present case,
 non-zero entries are observed exclusively within the block corresponding to the |01⟩ and |10⟩ states,
 consistent with the Bell state identified above;
 within this block,
 the entries lying on the diagonal are positive,
 while those lying off the diagonal are negative,
 this specific pattern of signs being the distinguishing feature that separates the state Ψ⁻ from the state Ψ⁺,
 the two states sharing an otherwise identical distribution of magnitudes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The joint outcome probability chart,
 shown in the lower right,
 reports probabilities of 0.50 for each of the outcomes |01⟩ and |10⟩,
 and a probability of zero for the outcomes |00⟩ and |11⟩,
 consistent with the amplitudes reported above.
 It may be observed that this distribution is indistinguishable from the one that would be obtained for the state Ψ⁺,
 since the two states differ only in the relative sign of their amplitudes,
 a feature to which the measurement probabilities,
 depending as they do only on the squared magnitude of each amplitude,
 are not sensitive.
 The distinction between the two states is accordingly not visible in this panel,
 and is recoverable only from the density matrix representation positioned alongside it,
 this contrast constituting the principal illustrative purpose of the present figure.
\end_layout

