\begin_layout Standard
The two figures reproduce screenshots of the graphical interface generated by Experiment 4 at two different points within the same game session,
 namely following the second and the eighth,
 and final,
 successful match,
 respectively.
 Both share the window title "Entanglement Growth - Bloch Spheres & Concurrence Trend," and both are organized according to the same layout employed throughout this thesis,
 with the addition,
 in the rightmost panel,
 of a plot showing the concurrence obtained at each successive match within the session,
 in place of the outcome-probability charts employed in the preceding experiments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the first figure,
 corresponding to the second match of the session,
 the rotation angle θ is reported as 0.39 radians,
 a value determined by the number of pairs previously identified rather than by a random process,
 as is the case in this experiment.
 The corresponding concurrence is reported as 0.38,
 and the two Bloch vectors are each of length 0.92,
 a value consistent with a comparatively low degree of entanglement.
 The trend panel on the right displays two points,
 corresponding to the first and second matches of the session,
 showing an increase in concurrence from approximately 0.20 to 0.38.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the second figure,
 corresponding to the eighth and final match of the session,
 the rotation angle θ is reported as 1.57 radians,
 corresponding to π/2,
 at which the entangling operation produces a maximally entangled state.
 The concurrence is accordingly reported as 1.00,
 and the length of each Bloch vector is reported as 0.00,
 both vectors having contracted to the centre of the sphere;
 a residual marker is visible at the centre of the sphere associated with the second qubit,
 corresponding to the same vanishing vector,
 of exactly zero length.
 The trend panel on the right now displays eight points,
 one for each match of the session,
 showing a monotonic increase in concurrence from approximately 0.20 at the first match to exactly 1.00 at the eighth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Considered together,
 the two figures document the defining characteristic of this experiment,
 namely the progressive increase of entanglement over the course of a single game session,
 rather than its determination by an independent random process,
 as in the case of the preceding experiment.
 The correspondence between the shortening of the two Bloch vectors and the rise of the plotted concurrence curve,
 observable across the two figures,
 provides direct visual confirmation of the relationship between these two quantities discussed in the preceding sections.
\end_layout

