\begin_layout Standard
The figure reproduces a screenshot of the graphical interface generated by Experiment 5 following the identification of a matching pair of cards during actual execution of the application.
 The window is titled "Phase Entanglement —
 Correlator View," and,
 as in the preceding figure,
 replaces the two Bloch sphere representations with a colour-coded representation of the density matrix;
 in this case,
 however,
 the joint outcome probability chart employed elsewhere is in turn replaced by a chart of two-qubit correlators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The uppermost region identifies the generated state through the formula (|00⟩−|01⟩+|10⟩+|11⟩)/2,
 together with a concurrence of 1.00 and a noise index of zero per cent,
 confirming a maximally entangled state obtained from an ideal,
 noiseless simulation.
 The four complex amplitudes reported in the final line are found to be of equal magnitude,
 0.50,
 differing only in the sign associated with the outcome |01⟩,
 consistent with the stated formula.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The density matrix representation,
 shown in the lower left,
 exhibits a pattern markedly different from that observed in the corresponding figure for the preceding experiment:
 rather than being confined to a single block of the matrix,
 non-zero entries,
 of equal magnitude,
 are present throughout the entire grid,
 each of the sixteen cells being populated with either a positive or a negative value.
 This density matrix therefore corresponds to a state in which every one of the four basis outcomes contributes to the superposition,
 in contrast with the Bell states considered previously,
 in which only two basis outcomes were involved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The chart shown in the lower right reports the expectation values of the three two-qubit correlators,
 ⟨XX⟩,
 ⟨YY⟩,
 and ⟨ZZ⟩,
 corresponding to measurements performed in three different combinations of measurement bases.
 The correlators ⟨XX⟩ and ⟨ZZ⟩ are both found to be exactly zero,
 whereas the correlator ⟨YY⟩ is found to attain its most negative possible value,
 −1.00.
 This outcome is to be contrasted with the joint outcome probability chart that would be obtained for this same state upon measurement in the standard computational basis,
 which,
 on account of the uniform magnitude of the four amplitudes reported above,
 would assign equal probability,
 0.25,
 to each of the four possible outcomes,
 and would accordingly exhibit no discernible structure.
 The correlator chart shown here demonstrates that the entanglement present in this state,
 despite being maximal,
 is not manifest in the computational basis,
 and becomes observable only when measurements are performed in an alternative basis,
 specifically along the Y axis of each qubit,
 this circumstance constituting the principal illustrative purpose of the present experiment.
\end_layout

